{"name":"Bashful Smile","hex":"#f7d2c4","fetched":"2026-10-18","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bashful","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Shy; not liking to be noticed; socially timid.","Indicating bashfulness."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bashful"},{"term":"smile","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A facial expression comprised by flexing the muscles of both ends of one's mouth, often showing the front teeth, without vocalisation, and in humans is a common involuntary or voluntary expression of happiness, pleasure, amusement, goodwill, or anxiety.","Favour; propitious regard."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To have (a smile) on one's face.","To express by smiling."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/smile"}],"entries":[{"title":"Smile","blocks":[{"text":"A smile is a facial expression formed primarily by flexing the muscles at the sides of the mouth. Some smiles include a contraction of the muscles at the corner of the eyes, an action known as a Duchenne smile. Among humans, a smile expresses delight, sociability, happiness, joy, or amusement. It is distinct from a similar but usually involuntary expression of anxiety known as a grimace. Although cross-cultural studies have shown that smiling is a means of communication throughout the world, there are large differences among different cultures, religions, and societies, with some using smiles to convey confusion, embarrassment, or awkwardness. Smiles can not only be seen but heard when people talk."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Leonardo_da_Vinci_-_Mona_Lisa_%28Louvre%29.jpg/330px-Leonardo_da_Vinci_-_Mona_Lisa_%28Louvre%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smile","wikidata":"Q487"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Leonardo_da_Vinci_-_Mona_Lisa_%28Louvre%29.jpg/500px-Leonardo_da_Vinci_-_Mona